From 176b73cab9abf1de5221a772f7e271e0b16b1bc7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dag Sommer <dagaleks@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 15:35:53 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 25/25] net: bound the declared frame length before slicing to
 it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The length arrives on the wire as four bytes, so a proxy can declare up to 4 GiB,
and read_frame sliced the guest buffer to it unchecked. A disagreeing or buggy proxy
therefore panics the virtio-net worker thread and takes the VM with it. This is not
Windows-specific — it is live on Linux and macOS today, and the Windows backend added
in this series already rejects the same input, so the two paths disagreed about a
safety property.

Not a guest escape: the prefix is written by the host-side proxy, not by the guest,
so a hostile guest cannot reach it. It is a robustness bug, and the cost of fixing it
is one comparison.

Fatal to the link rather than recoverable, deliberately. Once the framing is lost
there is no way to find the next boundary in the stream without guessing, and
guessing at frame boundaries in a data path is how corruption becomes silent.

Also normalises rustfmt across the series; cargo fmt --all --check is clean and the
only files touched are ones this series already modifies.

Verified: 0 errors on Linux and Windows with --features blk,net, and krun.dll relinks
after deletion at 4,234,752 bytes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
